WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.620
Speaker 1: Another incredible moment during
yesterday's committee hearings.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:11.360
The January six Trump riot committee hearing
was when a rioter showed up, said he regrets

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.070
everything and explains that he was duped
by Trump.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:21.539
We're going to be looking at video here of
January six rioter Stephen Ayers, who testified

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:24.710
to the January six committee under oath.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:30.939
And one of the fascinating parts of this is
we've had this vague question, were the rioters

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:33.550
really paying attention to what Trump said?

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:40.280
So the defense that's often made as, listen,
a lot of these people, they came from far

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.579
away to Washington, D.C.

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:48.769
It couldn't possibly have been what Trump
said at noon that made them go and riot at

00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:49.789
1 p.m..

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:51.989
And of course, this is a very shortsighted
analysis.

00:00:51.989 --> 00:00:59.239
It's barely an analysis because the the sort
of incitement to that event started well before

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:00.629
January six.

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:04.820
But we're actually going to hear from Rioter
himself, Stephen Ayers tells us during his

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.830
testimony, Yeah, you know, if Trump earlier
had said, go home, we would have gone home.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.229
We thought that's what he wanted us to do.

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:12.430
So let's go to the first clip.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:16.530
How did he end up there and does he still
believe the election was stolen?

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:21.680
Because, remember, the reason for the rioting
was the election was stolen by Joe Biden and

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.140
we've got to take it back.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.649
Stephen Ayers was asked about this interesting
answer.

00:01:25.649 --> 00:01:30.360
Speaker 2: You know, that's basically what
got me to come down here.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.450
And do you still believe the election was
stolen?

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.649
Not so much now.

00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:37.950
I got away from all the social media.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:42.740
When January six happened, I basically deleted
it all.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.609
You know, I started doing my own research
and everything and.

00:01:45.609 --> 00:01:47.299
For me, for me.

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:48.920
Speaker 1: For me.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.420
I mean, his own research got him into this
problem in the first place, but it's still

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:53.420
interesting to listen to.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:54.689
Speaker 2: It got to be that.

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:58.679
To actually for that to actually take place
would be it's too big.

00:01:58.679 --> 00:02:03.630
You know, there'd be there's no way to keep
something like that quiet as big as something

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.929
like that.

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:10.040
You know, with all the you know, all the lawsuits
being shot down one after another, that that

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.120
was mainly what convinced me.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:18.170
Speaker 1: So this is someone who either has
been coached or is willing to see a reason.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.710
I mean, everything he's saying is completely
true.

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:25.190
The problem is we knew all of this on January
5th of 2021.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.530
60 lawsuits went nowhere.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.850
That information was available on January
5th before anyone decided to show up, before

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.120
anyone actually showed up and right it, maybe
they had decided already.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:41.250
The fact that such a conspiracy would require
so many moving parts and so many people to

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.250
be involved.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:50.100
It was election officials and precinct monitors
and state officials and federal people coordinating

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.400
and the people controlling the machine.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.290
Speaker 3: We're doing a class action lawsuit
against all machines.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:59.760
Speaker 1: Yeah, that analysis was available
to Stephen Ayers on January 5th.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.000
So listen, this guy is in trouble.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.080
He potentially has been coached, but it's
still interesting to hear what he had to say.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.080
Now.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:14.180
Later, Ayers testified that when Trump said
on Twitter, It's time for you to go.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.180
They decided to go.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.640
They were, as far as they believed, doing
whatever Trump wanted them to do.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.260
Speaker 3: Tweet I believe that included a
video telling people to go home.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.680
Did you see that and did that have any effect
on what you were doing.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.460
Speaker 2: When we were there?

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:35.210
As soon as that come out, everybody started
talking about it and it seemed like it started

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.210
to disperse.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:41.320
You know, some of the crowd, obviously, you
know, once we got back to the hotel room,

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:45.280
we seen that it was still going on, but it
definitely dispersed a lot of the crowd.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.710
Speaker 3: And did you leave at that point?

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:47.710
Speaker 2: We did.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:48.710
Yeah, we left.

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:54.290
Speaker 3: So in other words, that was the
key moment when you decided to leave, when

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.520
President Trump told people to go home?

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.890
Speaker 2: Yeah, yeah, we left.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.030
Right when that come out.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.730
Speaker 1: So very clear.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:04.080
They were there because of Trump.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.090
And when Trump said go home, they left it
very hard to argue that Trump had nothing

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.210
to do with this.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:14.910
He actually said Stephen Ayers did later in
his testimony.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.509
If Trump had tweeted to leave earlier, they
wouldn't even have been there in the first

00:04:18.509 --> 00:04:19.509
place.

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:23.270
Speaker 2: Yeah, I think everybody thought
he was going to be coming down.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:27.430
You know, he said in his speech, you know,
it's kind of like he's going to be there with

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:28.430
us.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.430
Wow.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.250
I mean, I think I believe that.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:36.780
Speaker 4: We know that you illegally entered
the Capitol that afternoon and then left the

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.650
Capitol area later on.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.030
What made you decide to leave?

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:45.360
Speaker 2: Basically, when President Trump
put his tweet out.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.520
We literally left right after that come out.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.310
You know.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:57.669
To me if he would have done that earlier in
the day, 130, you know that we wouldn't be

00:04:57.669 --> 00:04:58.669
in this.

00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:02.449
Maybe we wouldn't be in this bad of a situation
or something.

00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:07.360
Speaker 1: Whatever you think about Stephen
Ayers, whatever his intentions are now.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.009
And listen, I'm right there with you.

00:05:09.009 --> 00:05:12.509
This guy has every he's going to tell us in
a moment in the next clip.

00:05:12.509 --> 00:05:13.509
His life is ruined.

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:14.509
He lost his job.

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:16.039
He had to sell his house.

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:22.830
He has an interest in portraying himself as
the reformed, clear thinking former Trump

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.960
guy who denounces everything.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.669
No question about it.

00:05:26.669 --> 00:05:32.469
It's abundantly clear that they were paying
attention to what they believed Donald Trump

00:05:32.469 --> 00:05:33.469
wanted.

00:05:33.469 --> 00:05:36.759
Trump made it seem like he was going to march
with them down to the Capitol.

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:38.530
Stephen Ayers testified to that as well.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:39.530
All right.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:40.720
Last clip here.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.800
Ayers says this has ruined his life and that
makes sense.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:45.889
Do we feel bad?

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:46.960
Well, you tell me.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.009
I mean, if you believe in personal responsibility,
he made these decisions.

00:05:51.009 --> 00:05:55.999
Speaker 3: Looking back on it now, how do
you reflect on the role that you played in

00:05:55.999 --> 00:05:58.379
the crowd that day and what is going on in
your life?

00:05:58.379 --> 00:06:10.379
Speaker 2: Basically, you know, I lost my
job since this all happened, you know?

00:06:10.379 --> 00:06:13.749
Pretty much sold my house.

00:06:13.749 --> 00:06:16.180
So everything that happened with the charges.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:22.969
You know, thank God a lot of them did get
dismissed because I was just holding my phone,

00:06:22.969 --> 00:06:25.190
but at the same time, I was there.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:31.050
So, I mean, it definitely it changed my life,
you know, and not for the good.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.729
Definitely not for the, you know, for the
better.

00:06:33.729 --> 00:06:35.210
Yeah.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:36.500
I mean.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:44.970
Speaker 1: Well, so whatever your thought
is on how honest Stephen Ayers was in the

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:51.979
testimony or whatever else the important things
here are, number one, these people were genuinely

00:06:51.979 --> 00:06:54.039
duped by Trump and the people around him.

00:06:54.039 --> 00:06:56.000
Now we can say we don't care.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.699
We can say they deserved it.

00:06:57.699 --> 00:06:59.550
We can say that it was their responsibility.

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:00.570
These are adults.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:01.840
I'm with you.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.370
But they were duped by Trump.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:08.779
And so the idea that Trump was an irrelevant
player here, absolutely untrue.

00:07:08.779 --> 00:07:14.620
Secondly, the idea that had Trump called them
off earlier, they would have left at least

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:19.710
Stephen Ayers is saying, of course, we were
we were following what we understood to be

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.229
Donald Trump's direction.

00:07:21.229 --> 00:07:24.740
And that is another key component here.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:27.080
So a fascinating day.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:32.560
And it is expected that next week there's
going to be another blockbuster day of testimony,

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.449
which we will, of course, be covering for
you on the podcast and potentially live as

00:07:36.449 --> 00:07:37.479
well.

00:07:37.479 --> 00:07:40.419
Let me know your thoughts about Stephen Ayers
as a witness.

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:41.750
Smart to bring him.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:42.750
Not smart.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:43.750
Did we learn anything or not?

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.029
I think it was an absolutely great decision.

00:07:46.029 --> 00:07:48.309
Some people emailed me, said I didn't get
the point of this testimony.

00:07:48.309 --> 00:07:49.660
Oh, I got the point.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:53.540
The point was was very clear, and I think
it was a good decision.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:54.540
Let me know.

